  "id": "vujmpel",
  "title": "Chapter 43: Planning in India",
  "order": 43,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bgpiucv",
      "content": "Economic planning refers to the systematic use of resources by the government to achieve specific economic and social objectives. In India, economic planning was introduced to promote rapid economic growth, reduce poverty, increase employment and achieve balanced regional development."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "Planning Commission",
          "value": "1950",
          "icon": "calendar"
        },
        {
          "label": "Five-Year Plans",
          "value": "12",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "NITI Aayog Formed",
          "value": "2015",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "Approach Shift",
          "value": "Bottom-Up",
          "icon": "check"
        }
      ],
      "id": "n748v2w"
    },
    {
      "id": "jvntr2t",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Meaning of Economic Planning"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Economic planning in India was aimed at achieving growth, modernization, self-reliance, and social justice.",
        "The Planning Commission was set up in March 1950 as an extra-constitutional and non-statutory body headed by the Prime Minister.",
        "India completed 12 Five-Year Plans between 1951 and 2017 before transitioning to a new planning framework.",
        "NITI Aayog (National Institution for Transforming India) replaced the Planning Commission on January 1, 2015.",
        "NITI Aayog serves as a think tank promoting cooperative federalism and a bottom-up approach to national development."
      ],
      "id": "b6pajge"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8l6u4lz",
      "content": "• Economic planning is the process through which the government decides how resources will be used."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3yb5jwm",
      "content": "• It involves setting targets and policies for economic development."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "x35x47i",
      "content": "• It aims to achieve balanced growth and social welfare."
    },
    {
      "id": "pn4b78q",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Objectives of Economic Planning in India"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b8mj2fz",
      "content": "• Economic growth and development."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "htilff9",
      "content": "• Reduction of poverty and unemployment."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "22dwzds",
      "content": "• Modernization of economy."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hd7n3kx",
      "content": "• Self-reliance and economic independence."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mzecedr",
      "content": "• Balanced regional development."
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "NITI Aayog serves as the premier policy think tank of the Government of India, providing directional and policy inputs.",
      "id": "ipegm79"
    },
    {
      "id": "k87bpcq",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Planning Commission"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qn2x12q",
      "content": "The Planning Commission was established in 1950 to formulate Five-Year Plans for India's economic development. It worked under the chairmanship of the Prime Minister."
    },
    {
      "id": "pfuf7za",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "content": "Functions of Planning Commission"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "j1b891u",
      "content": "• Formulate Five-Year Plans."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6rdcqtl",
      "content": "• Assess resources of the country."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "sxsk44h",
      "content": "• Determine priorities for development."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "etkouvd",
      "content": "• Monitor implementation of plans."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Planning Commission",
        "NITI Aayog"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Approach",
          "Top-down approach",
          "Bottom-up approach"
        ],
        [
          "Financial Allocation",
          "Allocated central funds to states and ministries",
          "Advisory think tank; no power to allocate funds"
        ],
        [
          "State Participation",
          "States played a passive role via National Development Council",
          "States actively participate through the Governing Council"
        ],
        [
          "Structure",
          "Relied on full-time members with limited external experts",
          "Includes full-time members, part-time members, and domain experts"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "0r35r9i"
    },
